<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rewa Kantha Agency</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rewa Kantha Agency was a British political agency in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> that supervised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a>, called Rewa in Gujarati, and the Mahi, among them Rajpipla, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhota-udaipur">Chhota Udaipur</a>, Baria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunavada">Lunavada</a>, together with many small chiefships. After independence its states were merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a> in 1948 and are now part of Gujarat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rewa-kantha-agency.jpg" alt="Rewa Kantha Agency" width="320" height="274" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Goran tek-en, following request by Nizil Shah · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rewa_Kantha_Agency_in_Gujarat_during_British_India_1811-1937.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
